        </table-wrap>
        <p>The pattern of spread and host inflammatory response further differentiates these infections. <italic toggle="yes">Pythium</italic> spp spreads centrifugally through stromal lamellae to the Descemet membrane and limbus, provoking an intense neutrophilic reaction and tissue melt. Fungal hyphae, in contrast, cause granulomatous or mixed inflammation, progressing slowly through anterior and midstromal planes.&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Acanthamoeba</italic>&#x000a0;spp exhibits unique perineural spread, resulting in radial keratoneuritis and a ring-shaped infiltrate with a lymphocytic host response.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-181910.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;<bold>Table 3</bold> summarizes the patterns and characteristics of dissemination.</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-181910.table2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 3.&#x000a0;Patterns of Dissemination of Pythrium, Fungal, and Acanthamoeba Keratitis</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellspacing="2" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Aspect</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><italic toggle="yes"><bold>Pythium </bold></italic><bold>spp</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Fungal</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><italic toggle="yes"><bold>Acanthamoeba </bold></italic><bold>spp</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Spread pathway</bold></p>
</td>
